pd.merge() in Pandas

pd.merge() is used to combine two DataFrames based on a common column or key.

Think of it like a SQL JOIN.

Simple example

Suppose we have two DataFrames:

Customers

import pandas as pd

customers = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer_id": [1, 2, 3],
    "name": ["Sugumar", "Ravi", "Kumar"]
})


===

| customer_id | name    |
| ----------: | ------- |
|           1 | Sugumar |
|           2 | Ravi    |
|           3 | Kumar   |


===

Orders

orders = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer_id": [1, 2, 4],
    "amount": [500, 700, 300]
})

===

| customer_id | amount |
| ----------: | -----: |
|           1 |    500 |
|           2 |    700 |
|           4 |    300 |


====

Both have a common column:

customer_id

Now:

result = pd.merge(customers, orders, on="customer_id")

Result

| customer_id | name    | amount |
| ----------: | ------- | -----: |
|           1 | Sugumar |    500 |
|           2 | Ravi    |    700 |

By default, pd.merge() performs an inner join, so only matching customer_id values are included.

=====

Real-time example

Imagine your beauty parlour appointment system has two tables.

Customers
---------

customers = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer_id": [101, 102, 103],
    "customer_name": ["Anu", "Priya", "Divya"]
})

====

Appointments
------------

appointments = pd.DataFrame({
    "customer_id": [101, 102, 104],
    "service": ["Facial", "Haircut", "Manicure"],
    "time": ["10:00", "11:00", "12:00"]
})

====

Merge them:

result = pd.merge(
    customers,
    appointments,
    on="customer_id"
)

Result:

| customer_id | customer_name | service | time  |
| ----------: | ------------- | ------- | ----- |
|         101 | Anu           | Facial  | 10:00 |
|         102 | Priya         | Haircut | 11:00 |


====

Important pd.merge() parameters
1. on

Specifies the common column:

pd.merge(df1, df2, on="customer_id")

====

2. how

Controls which rows are retained.

pd.merge(df1, df2, on="customer_id", how="inner")

====

Common options:

inner   → matching rows only
left    → all rows from left DataFrame
right   → all rows from right DataFrame
outer   → all rows from both DataFrames
Example
pd.merge(customers, appointments,
         on="customer_id",
         how="left")

This keeps all customers, even customers who don't have an appointment.

====

3. Different column names

Suppose:

customers

has:

customer_id

but another DataFrame has:

client_id

Use:

pd.merge(
    customers,
    appointments,
    left_on="customer_id",
    right_on="client_id"
)

====

pd.merge()

        DataFrame 1
             +
        DataFrame 2
             ↓
       common key
             ↓
      Combined DataFrame

pd.merge() = Combine DataFrames using a common key.

